I don't see the length of the game itself as a problem. To me, the
problem are any indefinite (in the case of pudding farming, in principle
even infinite) abusable tricks. I don't care if people want to play
slowly or for many terms; I care that the game sometimes rewards mere
stubbornness more than strategy.
If you make it impossible to get an infinite number of any creature,
btw, there already will be a natural limit beyond which play becomes
unrewarding.
As for the lack of tension between the mid game and the Wizard, that's
inescapable in the current game. Creating somewhat stronger monsters
isn't the solution, either; preventing the player from turning into a
walking tank is. But for that, you'd need to seriously revise the skill
system.

